1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:07,520
Okay, so if you look at the state of open models at the top of the agentic coding benchmarks right now, they're pretty big.

2
00:00:07,680 --> 00:00:09,680
In fact, most of them are kind of huge.

3
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:15,108
You're talking about 500 billion parameters, a trillion, sometimes even multiple trillions.

4
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:20,420
Now that's great if you've got a data center, but it's not much use if you're trying to run something locally on your own box.

5
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:32,000
Well, yesterday a lab called Recursive released a 65 billion parameter dense model, trained from scratch, that they say beats every US open weight model on Terminal Bench 2.1.

6
00:00:32,238 --> 00:00:38,756
Now I've actually got this running on the ZGX Fury in my office at the moment, hooked up to DSH, the Deep Seek harness.

7
00:00:39,246 --> 00:00:43,040
So I'm actually going to put it to the test of how well this does for agentic coding.

8
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:49,156
The other thing, though, that's really interesting about this release is that they're being very open about some of the stats.

9
00:00:49,550 --> 00:00:51,840
So the whole thing cost $32 million.

10
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:54,880
And they've actually told us how they actually did it.

11
00:00:55,150 --> 00:00:56,480
Okay, so who are this group of people?

12
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:58,240
Because I hadn't heard of them before.

13
00:00:58,400 --> 00:01:07,120
A quick note: this is Recursive with an eye, not to be confused with Recursive, Richard Socha's startup, which put out the Flint model recently.

14
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,836
That's a totally different company.

15
00:01:09,358 --> 00:01:15,200
So Recursive is a fairly new lab, and their main business isn't language models at all, it's chip design.

16
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:21,732
The founders are the team behind Alpha Chip at Google, the reinforcement learning research for laying out chips.

17
00:01:22,190 --> 00:01:24,644
And the pitch for that company is sort of closing the loop.

18
00:01:24,880 --> 00:01:29,680
Use AI to design better chips, use better chips to train better AI.

19
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:32,560
Certainly a story we're hearing more and more often now.

20
00:01:32,878 --> 00:01:36,400
So why would a chip design company pre-train an LLM from scratch?

21
00:01:36,686 --> 00:01:40,480
Well, the answer is because they needed one to power their chip design platform.

22
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,040
And they wanted control over the whole stack.

23
00:01:43,214 --> 00:01:46,560
The model, the training setup, the servings, the whole works there.

24
00:01:46,766 --> 00:01:52,240
Now, what they've released is a general purpose version of the model that runs inside that platform.

25
00:01:52,430 --> 00:01:57,120
Obviously, their fully decked out AI chipmaking version is staying proprietary.

26
00:01:57,422 --> 00:01:59,120
And for me, I don't really mind about that.

27
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:02,240
That kind of makes sense with what their company is focused on.

28
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:06,628
And it's kind of cool that they've released this model and all the artifacts that they actually have.

29
00:02:07,040 --> 00:02:11,940
So clearly, a chip design company putting out a coding model isn't something that we're seeing every week.

30
00:02:12,270 --> 00:02:20,160
But when you read their blog posts, it's one of the more detailed write-ups of how a model was actually trained that we've seen from any lab in quite a while.

31
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:22,960
And that's a big part of why I wanted to make this video.

32
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:24,880
All right, so let's look at what actually came out.

33
00:02:24,974 --> 00:02:28,400
So the model is Ripple 65B Preview.

34
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:32,612
It's a 65 billion parameter dense model, so not a mixture of experts.

35
00:02:32,942 --> 00:02:38,788
It's pre-trained on 12 trillion tokens with support for up to a million tokens of context.

36
00:02:39,182 --> 00:02:41,920
And already there, there are a number of things that are really interesting.

37
00:02:42,222 --> 00:02:46,308
Well, 12 trillion tokens is huge by any sort of standard.

38
00:02:46,510 --> 00:02:51,588
It's certainly a lot less than what we're seeing a lot of other models actually being trained on.

39
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,160
They've also released the instruction tune model, which is what I'm going to be testing with DSH.

40
00:02:56,320 --> 00:03:05,760
They've released that in a BFloat 16 version, an NVFP4 version, and a base model as well, if you wanted to do your own sort of fine-tuning from scratch.

41
00:03:06,094 --> 00:03:11,012
And I gotta say that that's pretty nice because a lot of the labs are no longer giving you the base model.

42
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:14,852
And if you want to do your own post-training, that's really what you kind of want there.

43
00:03:15,120 --> 00:03:18,080
All right, another cool thing is it's Apache 2.0.

44
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:25,680
You can use it commercially, you can fine-tune it, you can pretty much do whatever you want with this with no custom license full of weird restrictions in there.

45
00:03:25,902 --> 00:03:30,960
If you don't want to run it yourself, it's going to be on a bunch of different clouds, including Together, OpenRouter.

46
00:03:31,214 --> 00:03:33,252
I think Fireworks AI is also coming.

47
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:35,760
Okay, so back to the headline numbers.

48
00:03:35,920 --> 00:03:40,640
Well, on Terminal Bench 2.1, they're reporting 80.52.

49
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:47,280
Reflections beam model, which is a 500 billion parameter model, scored 80.1.

50
00:03:47,662 --> 00:03:56,080
The MetaMuse Spark Open model, which I did a whole video about, that's 816 billion parameters, and that only scored 76.4.

51
00:03:56,366 --> 00:04:02,532
And there are a couple of others further down on that link that come from other labs and are huge models, but are scoring less than this.

52
00:04:03,086 --> 00:04:09,440
So a 65B model edging out a 500B model, that's going to get my attention pretty much every day of the week.

53
00:04:09,902 --> 00:04:12,288
But here's the thing, and you have to read the claim carefully.

54
00:04:12,462 --> 00:04:17,088
It's a US open weight state of the art, and that qualifier there is doing a lot of work.

55
00:04:17,486 --> 00:04:24,932
But before I get into that, one of the things I want to ask from you is that my editors pointed out that the majority of people watching our videos are not subscribed.

56
00:04:25,326 --> 00:04:32,768
And nowadays with YouTube's algorithm, it really is basically a game of luck if you're going to see the next videos we make, if you're not already subscribed to the channel.

57
00:04:33,070 --> 00:04:43,076
So as much as I hate to do the YouTube thing, if you find the videos useful, please click like and subscribe so the videos actually get in front of a lot more people and hopefully you see them as they come out.

58
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:43,584
All right.

59
00:04:43,662 --> 00:04:44,928
so back to that qualifier.

60
00:04:45,008 --> 00:04:46,528
So let's put it into perspective.

61
00:04:46,688 --> 00:04:51,808
If you read the announcement, you think that this is almost the best open coding model out there.

62
00:04:51,888 --> 00:04:52,848
And it's not, right?

63
00:04:52,928 --> 00:05:02,368
If you look at the Chinese open models and the numbers are quite a bit higher there, you've got D Seq Flash 4.1, which is reporting 90.6 on the same benchmark.

64
00:05:02,670 --> 00:05:12,036
You've got Xiaomi's Mimo 2.6 Pro report at 89.9, and Kimi K3 and GLM 5.3 are both around 88.

65
00:05:12,494 --> 00:05:19,488
In fact, I think there are roughly around 10 Chinese open models sitting at least 5 to 10 points above Ripple.

66
00:05:19,630 --> 00:05:23,648
Now, some people might say that some of those models are actually benchmarks and stuff like that.

67
00:05:23,808 --> 00:05:24,932
That may be the case.

68
00:05:25,326 --> 00:05:35,008
And one of the things that interests me the most about this model is one, it is a very big dense model, and two, that it kind of wasn't made specifically just for agentic coding.

69
00:05:35,088 --> 00:05:40,036
It was made specifically for another use case and just being repurposed for this.

70
00:05:40,430 --> 00:05:54,608
So while there are mostly big mixture of expert models out there, like DeepSeek V4.1 Flash, which is around 763 billion total parameters, the fairer comparison for Ripple, I think, is the closest dense model.

71
00:05:54,688 --> 00:05:58,928
And that would be Quen 3.827B, also Apache 2.0.

72
00:05:59,182 --> 00:06:03,808
If you've watched the channel, you know that I'm a big fan of that model, and that's scoring at 73.

73
00:06:04,206 --> 00:06:10,208
So Ripple is a bit over twice the size of that, but it is seven and a half points better on the terminal bench.

74
00:06:10,414 --> 00:06:18,180
The other thing to keep in mind here is that almost all of these numbers that Ripple's included are self-reported using different harnesses.

75
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:31,408
When Valves ran Terminal Bench 2.1 independently, models like Mimo 2.6 Pro, Quen 3.8 Max came back actually in the high 60s, well below what other vendors had reported.

76
00:06:31,694 --> 00:06:40,996
So if you saw the video I made about the DeepSeek harness there, you would have heard my comments talking about how everything is moving towards harnesses for getting the top benchmarks, not just the model.

77
00:06:41,294 --> 00:06:57,808
So generally the thing I would say here is that perhaps this isn't the best model, but what it is is the strongest small dense model from a US lab with open weights, a base model, a published recipe, and something that's very competitive with the Chinese models that are already out there.

78
00:06:58,030 --> 00:07:02,756
So if you're in the market for any of those things, this is definitely a model that you've got to check out.

79
00:07:03,022 --> 00:07:06,128
All right, so how did they actually do this for $32 million?

80
00:07:06,542 --> 00:07:15,460
Well, one of the things that I really like here is that they're clear that the $32 million is the full RD cost, not just the final training run.

81
00:07:15,758 --> 00:07:32,516
So while we've seen some really good figures recently from things like MIMO 2.6, talking about $2.6 million for the RL training run and from perhaps some of the other labs, this actually is covering all the experiments, the data work, the evals, the post-training, all of it.

82
00:07:32,878 --> 00:07:38,768
And it's interesting that they estimate it's about a tenth of what the previous open weight leader cost.

83
00:07:39,150 --> 00:07:42,340
Now that's their estimate based on reported compute deals.

84
00:07:42,542 --> 00:07:47,808
So you could take it with a grain of salt, but I gotta say it does ring very true to other things that I've heard as well.

85
00:07:48,142 --> 00:07:50,116
Anyway, there are three things that stand out here.

86
00:07:50,368 --> 00:07:54,020
The first is that they went for dense, not a mixture of experts.

87
00:07:54,510 --> 00:07:58,244
Pretty much every other lab lately has been going for the MOE models.

88
00:07:58,448 --> 00:08:03,248
Ripple's argument is that a dense is just simpler to train and simpler to serve.

89
00:08:03,408 --> 00:08:07,488
They report around 40% MFU through pre-training and post-training.

90
00:08:07,662 --> 00:08:12,288
And it's interesting that they didn't go for any custom kernels or quantization in here.

91
00:08:12,528 --> 00:08:15,044
For a small team, all of that makes a lot of sense.

92
00:08:15,406 --> 00:08:27,888
MOEs can give you cheaper inference per token, but you often pay for it in the infrastructure and complexity of actually having to have all of those experts and their weights in memory when you're actually serving it.

93
00:08:28,174 --> 00:08:30,468
And it seems that Ripple just decided that wasn't worth it.

94
00:08:30,688 --> 00:08:33,168
So the architecture itself is thin and deep.

95
00:08:33,248 --> 00:08:36,288
It's got 80 layers, which is kind of interesting there.

96
00:08:36,494 --> 00:08:40,196
They're actually using more attention heads than you would normally see.

97
00:08:40,462 --> 00:08:47,168
And to make up for that, they use more sliding window attention layers with a smaller model and fewer global parameters.

98
00:08:47,694 --> 00:08:58,976
And I can't help but wonder: is this a decision that they made based on gossip that they've learned from friends working on the Gemini team, perhaps working on some of the other Frontier labs as well?

99
00:08:59,246 --> 00:09:03,136
On top of those details, there's also a handful of recent tricks that they've put in there.

100
00:09:03,216 --> 00:09:09,776
So they've got gated attention, they've got no positional encoding on global layers, and none of these are new on their own.

101
00:09:09,966 --> 00:09:12,896
The interesting bit, I think, is really how they chose them.

102
00:09:13,166 --> 00:09:17,284
Which brings me to the second thing that stands out here, and that's laddering.

103
00:09:17,376 --> 00:09:36,896
They started from the Omo 3 model ladder from AI2, which up until about a year ago were the only ones that really sort of published full open model weights, training data sets, recipes of how they were actually building their models, but because of their funding, most of the time their models were actually very small.

104
00:09:37,230 --> 00:09:42,660
Now you can kind of think of this ladder as a series of small models at increasing sizes.

105
00:09:42,990 --> 00:09:47,376
So you can test a change cheaply and see where it holds up to scale.

106
00:09:47,662 --> 00:09:49,616
So they tested changes one at a time.

107
00:09:49,776 --> 00:09:51,216
Some worked, some didn't.

108
00:09:51,502 --> 00:10:00,256
They tried some variants of things like hyperconnections that looked really good at a small scale, but really did nothing at 4 billion parameters or above, so they dropped it.

109
00:10:00,526 --> 00:10:06,896
And I got to say, this is something that's really cool because you don't actually see labs publishing these kinds of things that often.

110
00:10:07,182 --> 00:10:16,900
To give you a little sidebar example, if you go and look at the architecture on the small Gemma models versus the bigger Gemma models, you will see they are different architectures.

111
00:10:17,136 --> 00:10:25,936
And the reason is because what often works at a small level doesn't work when you go up to a 12B, 26B, 31B size of a model.

112
00:10:26,254 --> 00:10:29,136
The third thing that I found really interesting in here is their post-trainings.

113
00:10:29,262 --> 00:10:31,456
So they didn't buy any post-training data.

114
00:10:31,536 --> 00:10:34,976
They built their own synthetic data pipelines and environments.

115
00:10:35,136 --> 00:10:39,136
And they kept adding harder RL tasks as the model improved.

116
00:10:39,406 --> 00:10:44,228
And they got that to the 80.5 with 1.4 million RL rollouts.

117
00:10:44,654 --> 00:10:50,372
And it's interesting that they comment that that was probably around 70 times fewer than being used.

118
00:10:50,926 --> 00:10:54,416
So I think the story here isn't some secret new architecture.

119
00:10:54,496 --> 00:11:01,540
It's a lot of careful small-scale experiments on only scaling up things that actually held up.

120
00:11:01,966 --> 00:11:11,556
And the other cool thing is that they've been upfront in that they've been building on the work from organizations like AI2, Prime Intellect, NVIDIA, and others to do it.

121
00:11:11,918 --> 00:11:22,816
And I think this is one of the biggest challenges that we're seeing at the moment, as the sort of big frontier proprietary model labs stay fully closed, don't publish any research.

122
00:11:23,150 --> 00:11:25,616
don't really give back anything beyond.

123
00:11:25,616 --> 00:11:29,856
just at best, an open weights model, which they don't tell you how it was actually trained.

124
00:11:30,030 --> 00:11:39,696
etc . makes it really hard for academia and for startups to find ways to actually make better models and improve the whole ecosystem as a whole.

125
00:11:40,014 --> 00:11:43,536
All right, so they claim that this can basically run on a bunch of different hardware out there.

126
00:11:43,696 --> 00:11:47,056
I'm sure we're going to see a bunch of different quantizations and stuff like that.

127
00:11:47,216 --> 00:11:53,296
Today I'm going to be running the NVFP4 version on the ZGX Fury, which is a DGX station.

128
00:11:53,614 --> 00:11:56,816
I should stress that this video is not sponsored by them or by anyone.

129
00:11:57,070 --> 00:12:02,256
I just feel like while I've got this machine on load, I want to put it through its paces for as many models as possible.

130
00:12:02,638 --> 00:12:06,372
Let's jump in and have a look at both the model and actually using it with the Deep Seek harness.

131
00:12:06,894 --> 00:12:13,376
Okay, so jumping into the demo here, I've already come through and run the Agentic Pro tool calls and stuff like that.

132
00:12:13,616 --> 00:12:20,096
All of that works as long as the model is configured right and as long as the serving setup is configured right.

133
00:12:20,334 --> 00:12:22,496
So initially I had some issues with SG Lang.

134
00:12:22,736 --> 00:12:25,936
Once that was fixed, everything was working really fine in here.

135
00:12:26,096 --> 00:12:32,336
So the complicated ones work, and not surprisingly, all of the easier ones work in here as well.

136
00:12:32,462 --> 00:12:36,016
And they're coming through very quickly, which is really cool here.

137
00:12:36,256 --> 00:12:44,736
So that's a full pass on all the different tool calling, all the different resistance to prompt injection and tool baiting and things like that in there as well.

138
00:12:45,166 --> 00:12:53,376
If we come up here and just run the test for writing a long essay in here, I think this is one of the things that I find interesting about this model in general.

139
00:12:54,048 --> 00:13:09,008
Is that because it seems to be pre-trained on different data and perhaps not just trained on the same stack that everyone else has gone for, it definitely has its own feel to it more than you know some of the other ones.

140
00:13:09,230 --> 00:13:21,008
So both for generating things like new essays and stuff out, or as in this case, where it's actually going through and critiquing an essay out that it generated itself.

141
00:13:21,262 --> 00:13:29,728
The Chinese models will often generate out a really nice essay, and then you get it to fact-check that essay, and it will claim that there were lots and lots of errors in there.

142
00:13:29,968 --> 00:13:36,100
This one finds a few errors in its own output, but it definitely seems more to the point.

143
00:13:36,302 --> 00:13:41,408
Now, to get there, it did use 10,000 tokens of thinking, you know, going through there.

144
00:13:41,568 --> 00:13:44,288
So that's something you definitely want to take into account as well.

145
00:13:44,528 --> 00:13:51,248
If we look at things like the standard rag tests in here, again, it likes to use a decent amount of thinking.

146
00:13:51,438 --> 00:13:56,740
Not a huge amount here though, but the answers out are very succinct and to the point.

147
00:13:56,928 --> 00:14:01,568
In this case, we've got 1700 tokens of thinking, but our output is very sort of clear.

148
00:14:01,870 --> 00:14:04,608
Again, for things like the logic puzzles and stuff like that.

149
00:14:04,768 --> 00:14:23,568
The one that I find really kind of interesting is, if we look at the SVG tests, so all the kind of Chinese models and a lot of the models nowadays seem to produce very similar SVGs of the sort of Pelican on a bicycle, or like when I do the red dragon on a bicycle, etc.

150
00:14:24,048 --> 00:14:30,928
And that really makes me suspicious, of, like, are they actually just being distilled for doing these kind of tasks?

151
00:14:31,022 --> 00:14:34,528
or what sort of training data are people using for these kind of tasks?

152
00:14:34,768 --> 00:14:44,208
One of the things here that I find, and I've run this about four or five times now, is that it really does produce out different looks.

153
00:14:44,398 --> 00:14:45,648
I won't say that they're better.

154
00:14:45,808 --> 00:14:55,844
Some of them are not necessarily better, but I do find that the look to them are very sort of original as opposed to what we've seen for a lot of the other models.

155
00:14:56,174 --> 00:14:57,728
Okay, so finally it's done.

156
00:14:57,808 --> 00:15:02,660
And if we come and look at it, we can see that the look of this one is very different.

157
00:15:03,008 --> 00:15:08,768
And when I've rendered them out, they're definitely not as good as some of the Chinese models for this.

158
00:15:08,928 --> 00:15:13,568
My guess is that this is not something that they've prioritized for really at all in here.

159
00:15:13,808 --> 00:15:20,368
The thing that I find really interesting though is sometimes we get like almost a monochrome version of the picture.

160
00:15:20,528 --> 00:15:25,648
They just have a very different style than what you see in the other models, etc.

161
00:15:25,998 --> 00:15:33,008
Okay, if we give it a design task like the Dario's Wellness Retreat, we can see that actually does an okay job.

162
00:15:33,088 --> 00:15:44,528
I wouldn't say it's going to win awards here for design, but compared to a lot of the other things out there, this is doing, you know, pretty good at being able to have all the elements and stuff in here.

163
00:15:45,006 --> 00:15:50,660
The thing I strongly suspect with this is that it just was never actually built for doing this kind of task.

164
00:15:50,928 --> 00:15:58,756
We look at the thinking, we've got good clear thinking in there for design, for doing things like the colors and managing, you know, all of that.

165
00:15:59,022 --> 00:16:11,888
But my guess is that if you wanted to use this model for this kind of task, you probably want to do either some more continual pre training or certainly a bunch of SFT for the design kinds of tasks in here.

166
00:16:12,270 --> 00:16:16,068
All right, let's jump into the Deep Seek Harness and try it out for different agentic tasks.

167
00:16:16,368 --> 00:16:22,288
Okay, to set this up with Deep Seat Harness, we can basically just come in and set up the model.

168
00:16:22,478 --> 00:16:24,448
I've got a custom model in here.

